 "name": "Light Colour and Wood Gloss in a Living Room: Pairs and Impressions",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Yu, Fan & Zhu (2026), \"Effects of Correlated Color Temperature and Wood Surface Gloss on Visual Comfort in Older Adults in Residential Interiors\", Buildings 16:2956. Six renderings of the same wood-finished living room (SketchUp 2022 + Enscape 3.5): 2700 K or 4000 K lighting × low, medium or high wood surface gloss. Participants compare all 15 pairs (random order and sides; fixation cross 2 s, pair shown 15 s, then choose the preferred room; 3 s blank), then rate each room in random order on six 7-point items: naturalness, warmth, relaxation, interest, desire to use, overall comfort. Analysis as in the paper: selection proportion per condition and 2 × 3 repeated-measures ANOVAs (CCT × gloss). Renderings are cropped from Figure 2 of the open-access paper (CC BY 4.0) with credit; the template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) eye tracking (Tobii Pro Fusion) is not recorded; (2) online instead of a lab monitor at 60 cm, so screen colour and brightness vary between participants; (3) the 15-second viewing time is enforced by enabling the choice buttons only after 15 s; (4) item wording and the scale end labels (1 = very low, 7 = very high) are written for this template, as the paper gives only the dimension names; (5) the post-experiment interview is replaced by one optional open question; (6) an \"Under 60\" age option is added because the original sample was aged 60–79; (7) images are about 1,250 px wide from the published figure.",
